f 17592186048472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050410 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050410 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050410 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050410 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050410 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050410 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050410 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050410 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050410 #edb/ref 1031 "Classifying Triangles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050410 #edb/ref 1033 1601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050410 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "46ce73a1-ea0d-553e-b5fc-67d8d59a64f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105525 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105525 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the measure of $\\angle{A}$ in the triangle $\\triangle{ABC}$ below?\n\n![](images/91/91d1c211d33bb42a474242a6895330a3a2b77b50450d8ba7a1b533cb0a45f3b9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105525 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df6c46c7-a65c-578c-a9b3-e75de73c527a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105525 #edb/ref 1074 "Since two sides are congruent, $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AB}$, we conclude that $\\triangle ABC$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = m\\angle A = x -10^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n(x - 10^{∘}) + (x + 20^{∘}) + (x - 10^{∘}) &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 60^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=60^\\circ$ into the equation for the measure of $\\angle A$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= x - 10^{∘} \\\\\n&= 60^{∘} - 10^{∘} \\\\\n&= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 50^\\circ\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105526 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105526 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the measure of $\\angle{H}$ in the triangle $\\triangle{FGH}$ below?\n\n![](images/b1/b13dd7712b6ed41bbd6ba9db85b489f71fb498c711d5dd67d4dd91a0845b5dcd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105526 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7ae19b8-aee0-5a64-b7c8-61f3ce75272c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105526 #edb/ref 1074 "First, from the diagram, notice that $\\angle{HFG}$ and $\\angle{EFH}$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠HFG + m∠EFH &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠HFG + 105^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠HFG &= 75^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\triangle{FGH}$ is an isosceles triangle where $\\overline{GH} \\cong \\overline{FH}$, the base angles must be equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{G} = m\\angle{HFG}=75^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since the interior angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠G + m∠HFG + m∠H &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n75^{∘} + 75^{∘} + m∠H &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n150^{∘} + m∠H &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠H &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105527 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105527 #edb/ref 1071 "In the figure below, $m\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ$. What is the measure of $\\angle{D}$?\n\n![](images/59/59bb54f508b8045eeffb7cdbf547b6be0f074009b3288fb13e115159909782a8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105527 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e44e7e25-f65b-5f40-9aef-cbe55bfd1b53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105527 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an equilateral triangle. Consequently, all of the interior angles must be equal to $60^\\circ$:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAC}=m\\angle{ABC}=m\\angle{C}=60^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, since $\\triangle{ADB}$ is an isosceles triangle, the base angles must have equal measure:\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAD}=m\\angle{ABD}\n$$\n\n![](images/a9/a997837f549d59b2a554bf6bc60d9de3ebf6b4b97188ac3d0f07342fb4af6507.png)\n\nNow, since we're told that\n\n$$\nm\\angle{CAD}=100^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAD &= m∠CAD - m∠BAC \\\\\n&= 100^{∘} - 60^{∘} \\\\\n&= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in $\\triangle{BAD}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAD + m∠D + m∠ABD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2m∠BAD + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2 \\cdot 40^{∘} + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n80^{∘} + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠D &= 100^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105584 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105584 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/84/842cd509d5d998858936dda5ae07cc17c69cfd83ea86881ba542d8d34bff7cc6.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ in the isosceles triangle $ABC$ above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105584 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9e953e1-5af0-55eb-a861-af6f4ed9b720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105584 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $\\triangle ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overline{AB}$, we have that $\\overline{BC} \\cong \\overline{AC}$. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= AC \\\\\n4x - 3 &= 2x + 1 \\\\\n2x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= x + 4 \\\\\n&= (2) + 4 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AB=6$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105738 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105738 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105738 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Measure of an Angle Using the Isosceles Triangle Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105738 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105738 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "02d46a44-0f02-5491-8e71-2eb22ad50ff9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105739 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105739 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105739 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105739 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Measure of an Angle in an Isosceles Triangle Given an Exterior Angle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105739 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105739 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0690d62b-432c-5d1e-96f2-b43393f6efa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105740 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105740 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105740 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105740 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving for a Variable Given Unknown Lengths in an Isosceles Triangle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105740 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105740 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c9c2b7ee-d32b-550b-b110-81d04d750c98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105741 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105741 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105741 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Measure of an Angle Given Multiple Triangles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105741 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105741 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0044ba61-80c8-57cf-80dc-8a17cf980a84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106021 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106021 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106021 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106021 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106021 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106021 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106021 #edb/ref 1045 "The Isosceles Triangle Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106021 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106021 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106021 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5207c8f6-7d65-5c9f-8bbb-af66fdcc9b1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106627 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106627 #edb/ref 1061 164419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5440fe87-c485-5fa7-ab27-be4de4746fa1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106628 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106628 #edb/ref 1061 164420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106628 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c37fcdd-4a4f-5fa4-b419-cdfb144ce00e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106628 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106629 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106629 #edb/ref 1061 164423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106629 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52480c3c-c289-5e97-b7aa-1a5401a73156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106629 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106630 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106630 #edb/ref 1061 164426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106630 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b5d08f57-ce6b-5494-a967-38442f05a2c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106630 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106631 #edb/ref 1061 164429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106631 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec111c1e-1c82-5a70-b2f1-5dfba6037c19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106631 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107043 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that longer sides in a triangle are opposite to larger angles, and vice versa. But what if two (or more) sides have the same length?\n\nAs you might have guessed, if two sides have the same length, then the opposite angles have the same measure. And vice-versa: if two angles have the same measure, then the opposite sides have the same length.\n\nSo, we obtain the following important properties of isosceles and equilateral triangles:\n\n- A triangle is **equilateral** if and only if all of its angles are of $60^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/ad/ad56532db83ae54eeabf04d5a182a850bed14d9d0a65518b8048c3c0c112c371.png)\n\n- A triangle is **isosceles** if and only if it has two congruent angles.\n\n![](images/2b/2b5d6779527eae10e108ffcdc80745f63f3f38e41474673990bf74c83d2e6a64.png)\n\n**Note:** The statement above encompasses two theorems:\n\n- The **isosceles triangle theorem**:*If a triangle is isosceles, then the base angles are congruent.*\n- The **converse isosceles triangle theorem**: *If two angles in a triangle have the same measure, then the triangle must be isosceles.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107043 #edb/ref 1065 2227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107043 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "87d68609-ccd1-5ebd-b129-2c6993e05b75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107043 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
